#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087546 :question/id #uuid "51ff19a3-5c58-5c26-9c29-14ffc7d284db" :question/math-academy-id "e-5304" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{x^2-x-6}{x^3 - 3 x^2 + x - 3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{({x}^{2}+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0{)}^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0b663c6-7296-5416-9279-89c8726a7169" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085657} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9114cb-d5f9-5e25-a50c-4703cac865b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085658} {:answer/id #uuid "f04f942f-4bb2-54f4-abe5-95b857ace3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085659} {:answer/id #uuid "0bbb273c-9bc2-55f5-8dc3-b1f51f218cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085660} {:answer/id #uuid "0cecd8b6-06dd-545d-b54e-9bbe30569873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 0, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085661}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a594d63e-4a4f-5e8e-b954-85fb8c9bd564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085656}] :question/id #uuid "658facf5-be39-5c7a-8984-576062c5d409" :question/math-academy-id "q-39927" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{x}{x^{3} + x^{2} + x}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2ee625a-e88b-5839-8eb5-cf2ea241dae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085777} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bc595a-a08c-502c-9e7f-3d082b50a538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085778} {:answer/id #uuid "04625b17-aa0d-540f-a752-c4761d00a508" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085779} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0fc964-47c0-5489-bcac-9754e4e48f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085780} {:answer/id #uuid "71472aac-8ea6-50d8-b51b-f50282c3497c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085781}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7a417943-12c6-5645-8b1a-de1e21902255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085776}] :question/id #uuid "9804f06a-3a1e-50fe-a8f6-ab3f28486a47" :question/math-academy-id "q-4695" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x) = \\frac{x^{2} - 1}{x^{3} - x^{2} + x - 1}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233172 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ce523e6-de7a-5c10-9dbd-54e117565b86" :question/math-academy-id "q-113547" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x+1)+(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+1}}{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233173 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e3a4d1b-ccf5-5eb8-a09b-9826eb08d075" :question/math-academy-id "q-113550" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{(x-3)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b06798-2160-5123-adeb-8fac4e25409f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244493} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb4901-52db-5ea7-b778-613db328f9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244797} {:answer/id #uuid "95e4ede5-330a-5d5d-b519-47ca590e8dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245627} {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246321} {:answer/id #uuid "ee4b0856-0eaf-556c-a63b-bf6bfda2eaae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1541557-1983-573e-b847-876677596fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246321} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce5440fc-dc76-5e79-91b4-ff690a389de3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246918}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "daec1f65-9c08-5df4-939b-bebb2395a749" :question/math-academy-id "q-113545" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}({x}^{2}+x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+x+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =1-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "341cefcf-6aa7-5dd0-8741-16a1e49c1126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324389} {:answer/id #uuid "b527320d-de25-5e52-98bb-3d68c7423f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325527} {:answer/id #uuid "d38f3c2a-a49e-5897-801c-424300e7c8a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325808} {:answer/id #uuid "d670e8d0-18bf-5af2-8bdf-5ce021e9152d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325837} {:answer/id #uuid "f34e9c7e-81fd-5622-b802-8d9eedc6bd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-7$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "341cefcf-6aa7-5dd0-8741-16a1e49c1126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324389} :answer-field/id #uuid "08795b3d-c8a6-5731-9194-35bf6c829513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326237}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f92ee8b-33fa-5343-9c16-73df2f465b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-39934" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x+3)}{x({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}(x+3)}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2x+8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+2x+8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x+8=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-4(1)(8) \\\\  & =-28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d801c1c-70ef-575e-a8ce-0cf042036ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345870} {:answer/id #uuid "af3b5cb8-178a-5fd6-856c-24bea81fccc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346879} {:answer/id #uuid "c858a68d-354f-5009-8590-cab42e410104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347095} {:answer/id #uuid "d8905902-e64e-5add-b785-c52f97aa4e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347258} {:answer/id #uuid "d89d09f7-625f-5ed9-981b-246d07b3adc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d801c1c-70ef-575e-a8ce-0cf042036ca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345870} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f66b65f-a57a-5bd8-9c0c-795c2010f044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347636}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7fbfa1c-696c-569c-8a32-b9f903615eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80696" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a272db-6815-53ec-87da-5a281c0b5932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396109} {:answer/id #uuid "81bc4acc-cf96-53dd-9604-97c51e416cf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396110} {:answer/id #uuid "d3972294-8bb0-593a-9b73-d4e09973c691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396111} {:answer/id #uuid "4649603f-3d59-5567-ab7b-c75a268bb6c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\sqrt{2},\\,x=0,\\,x=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396112} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3a157a-5fe4-53f2-9241-98666b5d0e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b3a157a-5fe4-53f2-9241-98666b5d0e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396113} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdc4a2be-1828-5c44-9645-c520108c6c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396108}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d87d550-5004-5d22-b9a3-b2335e96082d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6913" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{3}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9bdca2-12f3-5135-add9-f6cee60b6017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399804} {:answer/id #uuid "ea637b56-a1b6-530a-b8ad-0581d7725118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{3},x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399805} {:answer/id #uuid "6caf38b1-8d42-5322-9446-2276f113b03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399806} {:answer/id #uuid "88004c88-6f69-578a-840b-c594bbb79e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399807} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399808} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9f7db0-7e00-5308-89b8-2a0dcddb1466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399803}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a602f803-9ee4-55ae-ba4f-d88ab6ec6a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402697} {:answer/id #uuid "d1acf715-1373-5a06-b01b-ab3b303c535d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402698} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbc63c9-13bb-57db-8074-636542eaac49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402699} {:answer/id #uuid "66e7f76d-26a7-52c7-993f-99c8712a83c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402700} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad6dfaf-76c0-5356-9336-dc60a8e77b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402701} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e77536-5db4-551c-9973-483a9864ffa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402696}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10e486fb-a153-567a-b122-83eab72817c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-39936" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{{x}^{4}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{({x}^{2}-1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{(x-1)(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}}{\\cancel{(x-1)(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087599 :question/id #uuid "dc93ac2d-3588-5dc4-9cf8-3df1f17c353d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6599" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab721a7c-ebb0-5452-b247-5d3816198bf4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\sin x)y = 1 + 3\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084927} {:answer/id #uuid "b319152d-c291-5494-a461-4791daa6c062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (4\\cos x)y = 1 + 3\\sin x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084928} {:answer/id #uuid "c17d0099-f48a-51bc-b6bf-feeec39e8bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084929} {:answer/id #uuid "2a507547-4026-5e0f-ae6c-972cd79efc1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{1 + 3\\cos x}{2} = (2\\sin x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084930} {:answer/id #uuid "606289fa-36e0-57ab-b7c2-57b9dbbb4540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx} + (2\\sin x)y = 1 + \\frac{3}{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084931}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e534d32-a3b5-5b67-a30a-d8fb0702e472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186084926} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434} {:answer/id #uuid "eb82a4b2-58af-556c-a366-f92624768704" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401435} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a26a57-987c-5668-b531-e2f3db2b75bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=1+\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401436} {:answer/id #uuid "b6972414-a235-5987-855e-567ba571b605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)y=1+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401437} {:answer/id #uuid "e08cc03c-cea4-583d-b68b-e5c8622f76e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}=(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4bcf009-6fca-5110-8ae8-643678b3cb9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401434} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef647b19-79e2-5b54-b8df-1bf5fe9db9b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401433}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5c269fd-ead8-50e4-b16e-9d9e9b4a244c" :question/math-academy-id "q-16400" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088420 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d56ade3-8c2b-558b-8dee-d6c16408ae98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348562} {:answer/id #uuid "19d75fc4-d565-5247-a3d2-0dd1d71481b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348663} {:answer/id #uuid "33f582cb-d125-5a7e-bc13-6fd636ea22e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348913} {:answer/id #uuid "a15aa42d-fc0c-537c-988d-61613c47dc74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349879} {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e907ba10-aa76-5dbf-bb32-8f9829e3aeb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350489} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ee4a69-5c37-5b5b-9d83-fd6eb1f2f193" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350854}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fbd67f9-88e1-5554-8625-84ac1e940645" :question/math-academy-id "q-8359" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x-y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x-y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & +{{\\frac{1}{x}}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y={{2}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16600393-5943-5c7c-83fa-73f280d9f496" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{-2x}y-{e}^{-x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235954} {:answer/id #uuid "28fd1a90-bb3f-5f34-a198-d64604e7ece2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236074} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5f3f74-0a23-5401-aa89-9d12635660cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y-{e}^{-2x}x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236394} {:answer/id #uuid "71948203-de54-56d1-9f56-93d9391d2c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236538} {:answer/id #uuid "fca9fcb4-f28c-5421-8fe4-2f2bc1857dc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28fd1a90-bb3f-5f34-a198-d64604e7ece2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-2x}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236074} :answer-field/id #uuid "701c817f-730c-54ca-8fa6-f662db8b263d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237586}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee14253e-e208-56bc-9e49-57c918963c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-103349" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y-x & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{x}y & =x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(2{e}^{-x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{e}^{-2x}x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2{e}^{-x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{-2x}x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8ddc79-cd90-5a3a-92b9-66917add9af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236301} {:answer/id #uuid "84fa8218-25c6-5f9d-90b8-aac5aa59b9d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236644} {:answer/id #uuid "928963ed-bc3c-5514-8870-62a00c67947b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236747} {:answer/id #uuid "b883f316-044b-5d72-a310-71831ab4f8fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236997} {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdcd85e1-113b-566e-9ea7-1f2d13a7d310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237027} :answer-field/id #uuid "250ff475-83b4-5e4f-9a44-f3c56416dc2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237486}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b89344e-126d-544e-88e0-0303a3eae594" :question/math-academy-id "q-103354" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284437 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31bcc262-1642-5072-847b-f965c83d8d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282135} {:answer/id #uuid "6dee7921-ed08-59bd-8f27-4deeb4c2708b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-y=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282631} {:answer/id #uuid "721c80a1-3d3f-59bd-b10a-ec28f6a3a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282665} {:answer/id #uuid "78540369-9d2a-5cf2-9a31-e8d914b35aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282721} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa81d24-8383-5421-81be-7c245419023a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "721c80a1-3d3f-59bd-b10a-ec28f6a3a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}-xy=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282665} :answer-field/id #uuid "71914272-d79b-5413-b8f9-d3326e0d438c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "157e16d4-eb84-5272-a4e6-39b4e7ee7766" :question/math-academy-id "q-16396" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\ny\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={y}^{2}x\\,,\\,y\\ne 0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}x \\\\ y\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}x & =0 \\\\ y(\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}-yx & =0 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\,{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}-xy & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)=-{x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb601d3-81c5-5a96-8fc8-ebd341ceb45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281805} {:answer/id #uuid "1b28838c-5554-561f-8d1f-b421c2cfbbcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281936} {:answer/id #uuid "c21f19ed-5569-516f-809d-8429394e2585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+y\\,{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283377} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ad13ec-6b04-54a8-8096-a847fbcb5705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283535} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b0a236-a0ee-50c6-beda-f5362d816f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb601d3-81c5-5a96-8fc8-ebd341ceb45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dx}+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281805} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bbd239e-b8c4-5069-b4e1-e9313244312e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284143}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ce4583e-c99f-532c-8f96-a07269c60d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16401" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & ={(1-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2} & =1+{y}^{2}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & =1-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx}+{{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}\\,y & ={{1}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0646fdce-fd3c-56a7-8137-632d215be116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345404} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9afd84-fe8c-56a0-89e1-00f6ac764479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(1+x)y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346123} {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} {:answer/id #uuid "d204e6a5-02d7-5404-bddf-a96974b0383d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y=-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347190} {:answer/id #uuid "d895b1af-8150-5cb7-9f48-017f2ef90f34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71040d28-62bb-5d78-b4e6-2a190574112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346349} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ddf3aa-733e-5972-8973-46d76ff540e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347812}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f284d9d4-47c8-54c3-aa2b-11e7ba22d40f" :question/math-academy-id "q-79697" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x+y}{x}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x+y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{x+y}{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x(x+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}+xy \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-x$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348261 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345388} {:answer/id #uuid "139be62e-1605-5f69-b8d1-6b7a2554416d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345521} {:answer/id #uuid "3e94f02d-431a-5153-8655-00b4a87c74f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345883} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef9fe74-cdac-5d6d-8da5-effef6765981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+5{e}^{-x}y={e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346026} {:answer/id #uuid "50906fb5-5aea-54da-a635-e0e0c3771435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5xy=-{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "049ae29b-8ef2-515e-817d-95125832b9ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,-5{e}^{x}y=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345388} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ad19c6-417f-54a2-8eac-d9c3b03caa0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347862}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5a5eea4d-3d1c-5658-8171-517491bf7c77" :question/math-academy-id "q-79699" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y,\\,y\\ne 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ do not depend on $y.$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -y{e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}^{2}-y & =0 \\\\ -y({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({e}^{-x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y+1) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y{e}^{x}+{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,+{{(-5{e}^{x})}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-{e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5{e}^{x}y & =-{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-5{e}^{x}$ and $Q(x)=-{e}^{x}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404259 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d23ea584-4a5e-5d3f-b114-caef1a56d6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404261} {:answer/id #uuid "178d8403-eb54-593d-abc3-5b52a35b7885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404262} {:answer/id #uuid "ee26e4a3-b2b4-56a4-a778-0012ee7e2935" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404263} {:answer/id #uuid "38e4fc3c-40eb-5ddc-a361-e415766dbedb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404264} {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0883a541-a5d0-5369-94ee-df510266f33b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404265} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d75aab-799f-5138-9f8f-42b050f51ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404260}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef24453f-f045-5d0a-8ed3-0ca5b930bc74" :question/math-academy-id "q-8360" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=0\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{2(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087600 :question/id #uuid "2a6d7a3a-38a4-5925-8d1a-3d85b74ad350" :question/math-academy-id "e-6600" :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba1699a-1266-5d2a-83a1-9ef98174bfab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412769} {:answer/id #uuid "1d712324-050d-5573-a4a5-5a0cca8602dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}y=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412770} {:answer/id #uuid "346b10b5-2bec-56ce-ae2c-3edb9c4195a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4xy=\\sqrt{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412772} {:answer/id #uuid "4da0e1b1-496c-56ae-a84c-c4de8d1b4756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412773} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d36e89-9f24-5514-94a7-4cfb223701bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4da0e1b1-496c-56ae-a84c-c4de8d1b4756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412773} :answer-field/id #uuid "05fa89b3-8d8c-5d7d-a143-81330cce5025" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412775}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d7eb342-85c3-52e0-b57f-d62a1be729ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-103560" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{y-1}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\frac{1}{y-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088418 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e6e851-77d9-50d0-b99d-b8ca4b35fe00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349690} {:answer/id #uuid "bd11a2c4-956c-5921-bf71-eef3b4403859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350126} {:answer/id #uuid "d27ff332-32be-52d1-8f0c-1c58dea87e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350296} {:answer/id #uuid "ee885d63-62c6-5299-8069-ab09eb83e825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2x{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350531} {:answer/id #uuid "fede89cf-752a-5d9b-a4c1-e5ef628a1c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2xy=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d27ff332-32be-52d1-8f0c-1c58dea87e89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350296} :answer-field/id #uuid "e71f2d30-074a-5e70-868b-367fb755a8c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351114}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6eeef64-0029-5193-805e-25131224aaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-8349" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=6{x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8dc75e-3cf3-5795-857d-b484a8bface7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348706} {:answer/id #uuid "43bb70a4-1ebc-568d-ac4e-2b15cf3ae1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349062} {:answer/id #uuid "9c0edda0-970f-571f-af28-8b2bb516b055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349837} {:answer/id #uuid "aa32fc93-bb6e-5db9-9787-457ed8b63eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349959} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e4844b-3007-5cd6-b780-6e847081831e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43bb70a4-1ebc-568d-ac4e-2b15cf3ae1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349062} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf81e7df-70d6-59a2-9d91-cf73151d372e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351070}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ecc44493-c978-58e2-96eb-083d048c8f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-8352" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x=y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{-1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation cannot be written in standard form since the power of $y$ in the term $x{y}^{-1}$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238023 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f53d0fb-f21f-5d85-9b8f-a7a4b136209c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236015} {:answer/id #uuid "2ebad11d-35cc-5957-b0cd-d331da650b2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236113} {:answer/id #uuid "b02b6c4a-2dc7-5619-8048-51c5cb39616c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236936} {:answer/id #uuid "b628a64b-bad4-555c-8a56-2de7f107366f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236969} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8ca442-9f52-5b49-8b9a-0d2e18880c58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{3}y=\\frac{x}{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f53d0fb-f21f-5d85-9b8f-a7a4b136209c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236015} :answer-field/id #uuid "72d740d7-f970-555c-93ca-f0630f56e033" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237592}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9d6d25b-8b57-5b3f-a558-7b77221cef78" :question/math-academy-id "q-103551" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "300b7028-8560-5889-820e-67bb7d82637c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{x}^{3}=\\sqrt{1+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236124} {:answer/id #uuid "424e1545-6d6e-5b8b-a579-6b2fe9723368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{e}^{xy}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236240} {:answer/id #uuid "4aad0d63-4d3a-5f79-90c3-39b4c6066d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236296} {:answer/id #uuid "4eeff264-a800-50f0-92db-506e7eed8ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236324} {:answer/id #uuid "f71e6f13-79fb-5eaa-9b80-7655ba53b105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)={x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aad0d63-4d3a-5f79-90c3-39b4c6066d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}y={x}^