rd form since the power of $y$ in the term $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ is not $1.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2ecae5-1edd-578c-a948-26f405bb9e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345727} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf9857a-0582-583a-aa29-684f400ceb81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345741} {:answer/id #uuid "715a5cd9-0207-5cfa-93a5-10f6cfdf7202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346352} {:answer/id #uuid "82151a50-8f46-524b-8764-de2213dbd44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346489} {:answer/id #uuid "b816f520-9402-5b83-9b02-c363072b31e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82151a50-8f46-524b-8764-de2213dbd44f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346489} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb7594f9-73f0-5ae8-8637-57d291faea9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348019}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "876f8272-d67c-527c-9e50-16e055f2bb7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79750" :question/problem "Which of the following is **NOT** a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the right-hand side depends on $y.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "602a1896-949c-5ba4-acec-b55a0e8ffb0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346182} {:answer/id #uuid "70fc76d6-b92e-56b8-919f-75c301dc22b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}={e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346347} {:answer/id #uuid "7916f873-18e8-572e-8bfe-c7eb07bc42b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346412} {:answer/id #uuid "fe95e83e-7f9c-5f03-be67-6f0472843ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347590} {:answer/id #uuid "feb69fd7-991c-5452-a24a-67e0a548634f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe95e83e-7f9c-5f03-be67-6f0472843ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value 