7e45a-263e-5bda-a1f2-c7311c60bd2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346878} {:answer/id #uuid "d5db124f-5fbb-5ee0-ba29-fedcd2002552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{(xy)}=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc06d89-f31e-5c76-8552-7a0c27b6fa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346577} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fcdbbd5-f55e-534a-b1ce-604a533dab7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347805}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70cd3eea-ad7c-56e9-b2e7-84eddd1e158d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79753" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\sqrt{x})y=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\n$$\n\nis already is written in standard form with  $P(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $Q(x)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} {:answer/id #uuid "50784efb-ac52-5b2a-930e-224a3c46e1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346039} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7e5b5f-00cc-57b5-b9a0-eabad58dd3f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346165} {:answer/id #uuid "a42c961b-9393-58eb-a38e-3ff82db15d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346775} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e532ee-210c-54c5-b6ba-20b24eb7144c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b63f23d-3741-54dc-9da9-b5e686f502a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bd8f24-231f-5b7f-ad71-f5ad51631cdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347926}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c92a3ce0-666c-5499-94f8-c31ec02af3a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79754" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eee3b1f-7860-522c-8e25-2ab9122c7049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} {:answer/id #uuid "5eefff0b-47f1-50f6-aa20-45a25564434f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{e}^{2x}y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397783} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbcb751-5dd5-5c6c-8ef4-1f0333f1da29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{y}={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397784} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c9d281-4555-5a44-af3e-d9d7b6b8f65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397785} {:answer/id #uuid "54197164-398c-585a-83dd-5174235ce646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eee3b1f-7860-522c-8e25-2ab9122c7049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397782} :answer-field/id #uuid "440a36ad-3e7f-5834-9223-8af4fce0e717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "420cf089-e2ca-55d0-b4d5-3e91f54aadf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-103567" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =y\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-x{e}^{2x}y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-x{e}^{2x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087455 :question/id #uuid "6f249bfa-e57b-5784-9da8-d0f9aff213bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-21813" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "739a8f10-413a-5a1d-a47e-4afd0b14eb44" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c2b1967-4af5-520c-811d-5f2fb33cc469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302736} {:answer/id #uuid "7b84a815-9470-55cc-8164-a6fb81f55251" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303223} {:answer/id #uuid "e562f968-7867-523a-a569-53df8d43088c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303880} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d4c2cd-7975-5587-badc-859966cb0b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303891} {:answer/id #uuid "ebebf99c-d5e5-53b4-97ef-d68b99aecdfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e562f968-7867-523a-a569-53df8d43088c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303880} :answer-field/id #uuid "3326193e-0a0c-59c1-ad04-260c0af220a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304210}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "591724df-60a1-5a9d-9f71-6942e86af15b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331601" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=4$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecfc9ad-0591-5d56-b725-3982bf5bcba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302848} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c5df2b-0e09-5631-978d-a7638ab3eb56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303481} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d10eb5-5969-5f1c-9bba-1b1eb48631dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303605} {:answer/id #uuid "e7884954-9537-5ded-91a1-1196e28f05f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303895} {:answer/id #uuid "ec041024-752a-551d-bc1a-6d7c54f985c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecfc9ad-0591-5d56-b725-3982bf5bcba9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302848} :answer-field/id #uuid "f764d5e3-0408-5d5f-b201-353145c06dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304739}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7723c02-822f-5a83-b3af-429d938426d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-331744" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} {:answer/id #uuid "a4be644b-3c50-5bb5-b637-a1dc0590c6bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303470} {:answer/id #uuid "be5348b7-6521-531c-9590-b40f2257782f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303623} {:answer/id #uuid "bface693-37fe-568c-a4fa-08c4b3ba5757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303630} {:answer/id #uuid "e424eab9-c2e2-5989-8b94-f4410f99f5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb94a6c-9561-562c-9670-aaa9cd3544c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303430} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd3bff16-f60d-5153-a071-915cdea9c3fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304634}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331605" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcddfdb-c38a-5f92-b718-28036cb5e0a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302637} {:answer/id #uuid "24531df4-5849-5e8d-b717-57d07a8550b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302686} {:answer/id #uuid "5a15be87-c9e8-5225-a673-4b26b316be25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303004} {:answer/id #uuid "66137ed2-2b14-58d0-bd8a-ff3d608e1ddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303074} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c78ae-d3bd-501f-a813-f2ac8d3bb8c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303929} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d947d0-6f8d-54b2-932e-06f1d5e06c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304130}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ed9d4c5-c8ba-5702-acc9-702d6e5cd248" :question/math-academy-id "q-331724" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{2}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2{x}^{3}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2{x}^{3}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7603a78a-aca0-51e5-9c68-1571861f143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303180} {:answer/id #uuid "9a9253d0-497c-5ed8-9904-752fb5b447a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303413} {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} {:answer/id #uuid "b8164d95-12bd-5b59-aa2d-33ca3cd95e6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303598} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b02410-a499-5764-8c6c-bc4fea9102cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b05dbfdd-e018-5e06-8dd9-8f0ed1cd2926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303543} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f51f02b-20a7-5356-a7bf-6883df31265e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304251}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c450ce-db9f-5997-8c48-e9942b8b8389" :question/math-academy-id "q-331725" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7684d3-767a-52e5-bcc5-d780b7faa6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302753} {:answer/id #uuid "51c3b1e8-d753-5298-9a6f-e67e6cb118db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302962} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3595a2-a38b-57d0-aaa4-4e8fbbaa1590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303225} {:answer/id #uuid "da2cbaaf-6e7b-5962-ac89-10a2621a85d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303814} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8fad90-31aa-5644-9fc2-5632bf70c90a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3595a2-a38b-57d0-aaa4-4e8fbbaa1590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303225} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0510dd4-7330-5414-b48b-4c4f7e0d6ac0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304600}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb41985-a448-5edf-ac16-5140f9df994f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331727" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-3y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =-3y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y+3y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}+3)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6ad6bd-ebba-5732-80c2-8d1f7fefd916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302649} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce96722-6380-533c-ba73-d1d4dba3de7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303023} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcb8921-abbb-595e-8400-563faaf3eedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303421} {:answer/id #uuid "b27edf92-4fc4-5f2b-8e2a-0406162eac16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303563} {:answer/id #uuid "ee01f996-8937-58e6-8682-6e1145ce7e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bcb8921-abbb-595e-8400-563faaf3eedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303421} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4ac981-a1ea-5619-b6b7-e06c26ba211c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304492}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70f3dd1b-d16f-5aa1-9eba-10b4e0fd71e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331728" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2459a5ff-007e-530b-8edd-f46a552247c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4a8220-7054-525a-8afa-9194364f34cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303014} {:answer/id #uuid "86c7d469-f99c-5c11-aeb9-bf421146bc71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303299} {:answer/id #uuid "923a9172-9e8c-54b3-b41b-41a15e5952e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303364} {:answer/id #uuid "efe9d738-6b6f-52a9-a41b-35b9bd06d1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2459a5ff-007e-530b-8edd-f46a552247c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} :answer-field/id #uuid "c46c2ddf-b5c1-516e-be0e-d85055c76fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304611}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f8cfe21c-18ac-5f6d-9859-3a5cbe483542" :question/math-academy-id "q-331729" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}+7xy=5$\n\nII. ${y}^{′}+7xy=0$\n\nIII. ${y}^{′}+7xy=-2y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=5.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $5$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7xy=-2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+7xy & =-2y \\\\ {y}^{′}+7xy+2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(7x+2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0166a0e9-e475-5c8f-b032-9082b7fcde2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302458} {:answer/id #uuid "5314567f-e5df-5ca2-80e8-15b33f649506" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302971} {:answer/id #uuid "539ad669-6292-539a-9593-3f19bceb9427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302976} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f64220-da24-5e82-8ca4-f2181aec3314" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303555} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd819bc-3592-58f6-8e43-71f8e2d6858e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebd819bc-3592-58f6-8e43-71f8e2d6858e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303932} :answer-field/id #uuid "813a5976-379b-5f02-85ae-eb526a514659" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304427}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e88e764-3128-53c6-9d2a-d1f58dae12a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-331738" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304941 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085f0491-01b7-5bf1-9493-4de4eb3adc20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302513} {:answer/id #uuid "53fae90d-e1e5-5e33-bf40-bc28be9e851a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302978} {:answer/id #uuid "9e34058f-f878-5f33-8037-f52171e96c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303441} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dc1111-b6e1-50ea-8948-d3e287173f41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303980} {:answer/id #uuid "f4be8e3e-d3ca-5fac-a0d8-1f76a3eab20f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53fae90d-e1e5-5e33-bf40-bc28be9e851a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302978} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ba6d73-52f7-5082-b9b0-19271c2e615c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304609}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4b9025a-0c2a-511c-b23e-f617ee7031f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-331739" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-({x}^{2}+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304942 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0346d22e-99ca-51c0-8e3c-59b50a525a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} {:answer/id #uuid "1832ec2d-46da-5208-9b06-d9f7868bef40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302616} {:answer/id #uuid "97ace580-565a-598e-9cb1-40493c663d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303399} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a0b3a0-bdcf-5820-a95c-e2146d10bb23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303793} {:answer/id #uuid "db480e54-f880-5865-b053-131cc54e1a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0346d22e-99ca-51c0-8e3c-59b50a525a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302475} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5d3bec-6e2b-5dc7-98fa-15f9ea28173f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304452}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca0875e1-4252-5a69-bfc7-c43bf6770e82" :question/math-academy-id "q-331742" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3527a389-4846-52d0-a26e-ab7610b3c975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302786} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae9285b-64e1-54ff-a6e6-ae3a2dd4ef18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302929} {:answer/id #uuid "9079b25c-a77d-5d40-ad19-55fe4a678350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303359} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d4ad9a-fb93-5bbb-be30-9effc80dc1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303554} {:answer/id #uuid "e124e1e6-0f1a-55b2-8382-60e9817db497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e124e1e6-0f1a-55b2-8382-60e9817db497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186303860} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ec98d2f-0402-5a20-8b48-beacf10636a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186304293}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26251e42-10da-5a5f-8853-15a03bb82549" :question/math-academy-id "q-331743" :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=-2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous ODEs"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087877 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087452 :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abc22f1d-811f-5bdc-b37d-0282e04c8aec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085089} {:answer/id #uuid "da5a2b38-cb05-5079-8a67-44b3023a6a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\ln x) = \\ln x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085090} {:answer/id #uuid "011ca4e6-3122-58c6-a064-72bf61a7cc03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx^{2}}(yx^{2}) = x^{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085091} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc23e01-156d-58f7-838b-e84160a81d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{y}{x}) = \\frac{\\cos x}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085092} {:answer/id #uuid "6436279c-38d0-5c1d-a595-b7ab9c80521e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085093}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c0749d-dee0-5f31-b150-55643e10de7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085088}] :question/id #uuid "c7fefc62-82f5-5578-9142-a780040dc0af" :question/math-academy-id "q-17540" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos x$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088436 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a11d5a1d-e93d-5a5d-8111-e33d997e2841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087270} {:answer/id #uuid "e572ebc9-141f-578b-93ec-080a0deeb913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y \\cdot 2x) = 2x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087271} {:answer/id #uuid "fd667700-3e8d-53d0-9c71-264753a1f8cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx^{2}) = x^{2} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087272} {:answer/id #uuid "6e285ba0-0b5f-5e5a-a7bd-e5b3de551a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x^{2}}) = e^{x^{2}} \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087273} {:answer/id #uuid "76e91a95-c637-5848-86e4-e944f3eee17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x \\cdot 2xe^{-x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087274}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8fe340-5770-5791-befc-e802db4415ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087269}] :question/id #uuid "8973fb88-5098-5e4b-a0b0-25638565dd93" :question/math-academy-id "q-8546" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2xy = 2xe^{-x^{2}}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06e163d8-4a78-504d-8269-402a0415e08e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2x)=2x\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281750} {:answer/id #uuid "4382d319-73fb-5cb1-9dfc-25d3c057b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282265} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff9a224-fab5-5e91-b0ff-979e6a4e2c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282648} {:answer/id #uuid "bae778d3-7532-5194-9c25-d299082a3153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{2x})={e}^{2x}\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283301} {:answer/id #uuid "c179d6ec-3d07-5033-ad1d-7ca1e0d4284e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4382d319-73fb-5cb1-9dfc-25d3c057b161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282265} :answer-field/id #uuid "4288ef37-6c06-5c5f-a10c-1e0e96591b46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284042}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65a9092a-d6a6-5192-82ca-c04151a6438f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17533" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\nx\\frac{dy}{dx}+xy={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y=x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 1\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+y) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x \\\\ {e}^{x}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{e}^{x}y & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot x\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "217ccf45-8734-56ec-b06d-0fa3666f9d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281985} {:answer/id #uuid "610a3a60-2d25-5e70-9070-a40fd0b17bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282515} {:answer/id #uuid "692ebc86-2eeb-51e8-909d-911b7bbb78b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282591} {:answer/id #uuid "887e5de7-cd20-589e-9062-1ed36efe796c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282865} {:answer/id #uuid "99833182-f1d2-5cc1-b043-ddfe57635bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "692ebc86-2eeb-51e8-909d-911b7bbb78b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282591} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd94faa5-6de0-52b0-a454-db6562b0e660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284320}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0dcacbda-1dc5-5146-b0bc-75c7bab3748d" :question/math-academy-id "q-17539" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+xy={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63e2b089-092a-5eca-9f7e-bf626ffedb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} {:answer/id #uuid "71425229-5e0c-56e4-a3c5-6da6bfb56a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{x/2})={e}^{x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309627} {:answer/id #uuid "cd378351-ace0-5392-8e0c-b064ad6a0d66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2}))=-\\frac{x}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310393} {:answer/id #uuid "e71cd363-dab5-57e2-b1a7-f9cdab639247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}))=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310584} {:answer/id #uuid "f08a16be-ff29-5897-9836-28db90a5052f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2)=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63e2b089-092a-5eca-9f7e-bf626ffedb03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2})={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309516} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03a098b-971d-55f6-a30b-4a30fe70ddf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311307}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dba525fe-7e4d-5319-88bd-1b806feb0904" :question/math-academy-id "q-335278" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1/2)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{-x/2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{2}y) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{-x/2}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x/2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{-x/2}) & ={e}^{-x/2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205e27b5-4aef-53eb-8cf0-e7365d5f4c57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345637} {:answer/id #uuid "7510063a-6cf7-5663-bf6a-5afc7e2ed0eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346382} {:answer/id #uuid "8483449c-5ecd-5c41-92e1-0ded8b81ec2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346505} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c267a8-32e4-55c8-8af3-388216c0c46a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346964} {:answer/id #uuid "cf856d8b-bb28-5fc8-bf77-c77ea36fa1c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9c267a8-32e4-55c8-8af3-388216c0c46a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346964} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3a3107-9d3a-5db2-9a74-94dd3b070895" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347795}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7613e649-714b-58e4-9ccc-7a0b944dc47c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79818" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y={e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)={e}^{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)=x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot y) & =x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx) & =x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348295 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89cf29a8-7002-55ea-90d8-6e36bd328018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346547} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbc1949-1dc3-5132-b9a6-2f5a02db8b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346980} {:answer/id #uuid "c06aae51-d699-5f08-973e-040bd676bd09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347016} {:answer/id #uuid "d0fb694c-f9b1-5dce-86ee-87c1b3194d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347181} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d204d7-b947-5d35-bd31-8a40d4cc447b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot 2)=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbbc1949-1dc3-5132-b9a6-2f5a02db8b43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346980} :answer-field/id #uuid "b92de6c6-2fc5-5d10-b5ab-f609af76b489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347930}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2870a84c-606f-5fc5-a1ed-a94d670c16ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-79819" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\nx\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=1\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{2}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}\\cdot y) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b978ec0-c1dc-593e-85ec-c512eb8b8bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x|)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435832} {:answer/id #uuid "1da376ec-fadc-58b2-9050-98c3a0e3289d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435835} {:answer/id #uuid "39544074-ae02-530d-9e3a-2a0b0340a99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{2x})={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435838} {:answer/id #uuid "97ed5649-7d28-5597-8785-b502fbbd5161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\cdot 2x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435845} {:answer/id #uuid "a6707d7d-a941-50a8-9de7-4cedeafd40fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}})={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6707d7d-a941-50a8-9de7-4cedeafd40fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}})={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435847} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf5e5ce-2a24-563a-8eed-d37940cab8bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435863}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73fd6309-052f-5dd1-8e4c-82525a25d454" :question/math-academy-id "q-335274" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y=y(x):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+2xy=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))=I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=2x$ and $Q(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2x\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{dy}{dx}+2xy) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}+2x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\cdot y & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x)):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(y{e}^{{x}^{2}}) & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087878 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bbeb4f6-23b2-586c-8cb9-9742cce5a9a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3f6e10